From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 08:17:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: zuma-husky: enable hsi2c_11, the
 MAX77759 bus

The MAX77759 (PMIC, charger, fuel gauge, Type-C port controller) sits on
USI11 / hsi2c_11. The bus, USI11, CMU_PERIC1 and its SYSREG were left
disabled and a bring-up overlay switched them on. Move that into the
board file: the USB replug rescue in the husky device package needs the
bus, because the Type-C side opens its USB2 data switch on an unplug and,
with no Type-C driver, the phone stays off the USB bus after a replug
until something closes it again.

Binding the PMIC at 0x66 also creates its charger cell, and the charger
driver programs charge limits from the battery node. max77759_charger,
gpio_max77759, nvmem_max77759 and tcpci_maxim stay out of the way
through the husky device package's omamobile-max77759.conf (blacklist
only, a manual modprobe still works), so this DTB goes with that
package. The Type-C controller node stays disabled.

Update the comment that said the bus is enabled by an overlay and that
the package does not block the drivers yet.

Tested on a Pixel 8 Pro: the same four nodes were enabled by the
bring-up overlay for days; with them on, the fuel gauge, the PMIC and
the USB replug rescue work. This commit moves them into the board file
and was only built (W=1, dt-validate shows the two existing eusb2-phy
messages), not booted by itself.

Signed-off-by: Lef <me@lefteros.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts     | 28 ++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
index f9d30456a..38e9961a8 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/zuma-husky.dts
@@ -228,16 +228,29 @@ &usi2_hsi0 {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+&cmu_peric1 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&sysreg_peric1 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&usi11 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 /*
  * MAX77759 (charger, Type-C port controller, fuel gauge, PMIC) on USI11 /
  * hsi2c_11. Like the Pixel 7 Pro's, but the interrupt lines are on gpa7 and
- * gpa8. The bus and CMU_PERIC1 stay disabled here, and a bring-up overlay
- * enables them. Binding the PMIC at 0x66 also creates its charger cell,
- * and the charger driver then programs charge limits from the battery
- * node, so max77759_charger, gpio_max77759, nvmem_max77759 and tcpci_maxim
- * have to be kept from loading until each step is tested. The husky device
- * package does not block them yet (cheetah's omamobile-max77759.conf
- * does), and has to before an overlay that enables this bus ships.
+ * gpa8. The bus, USI11 and CMU_PERIC1 are enabled here: the USB replug
+ * rescue in the husky device package needs the bus (without it the phone
+ * leaves the USB bus on an unplug and stays off it after a replug). Binding
+ * the PMIC at 0x66 also creates its charger cell, and the charger driver
+ * then programs charge limits from the battery node, so max77759_charger,
+ * gpio_max77759, nvmem_max77759 and tcpci_maxim have to be kept from loading
+ * until each step is tested: the husky device package blocks them
+ * (omamobile-max77759.conf, as cheetah's does), so install it with this DTB.
  *
  * The fuel gauge has no battery model yet: the pack (ATL or COS, stock
  * reads its ID resistor) is not known, and a wrong model would be written
@@ -246,6 +259,7 @@ &usi2_hsi0 {
  */
 &hsi2c_11 {
 	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
 
 	usb-typec@25 {
 		compatible = "maxim,max77759-tcpci", "maxim,max33359";
